Slide 2: The Quality Shift (Old vs. New Mindset)
•	Estimated Time: 1 minute 30 seconds
•	What to Say:
"On the left, you see the traditional management approach. When something goes wrong, the instinct is to inspect harder at the finish line and ask, 'Who made this mistake?' In that world, quality is viewed as an expensive overhead cost.On the right is how a World Quality Leader thinks. Pioneering quality thinkers proved that over 85% of defects come from flawed systems, not bad people. Instead of inspecting defects out at the end, great leaders prevent them at the source. They don’t blame employees; they fix broken processes. And when you do that, quality stops being a cost—it becomes your biggest engine for growth and customer loyalty."
•	Transition: "So, how do these leaders build that environment? It comes down to five core pillars."
________________________________________
Slide 3: The 5 Pillars of Quality Leadership
•	Estimated Time: 2 minutes
•	What to Say:
"Every world-class quality organization stands on these five pillars:
1.	Customer Obsession: Quality isn't defined by our internal spec sheet; it’s defined strictly by what the customer experiences.
2.	Systems Thinking: Whenever an error happens, look at the workflow, tools, and training before looking at the individual.
3.	Data Over Instinct: Great leaders don't guess. They use root-cause analysis and real metrics to solve problems permanently.
4.	Psychological Safety: This is crucial. Frontline workers must feel completely safe to report a mistake the second it happens—without fear of punishment.
5.	Relentless Kaizen: Finally, continuous improvement isn't a once-a-year audit. It’s a daily habit of making things 1% better every single day."
•	Transition: "These principles sound great on paper, so let’s look at how real companies use them on the ground."
