Slide 4: Live Case Study 1 – Manufacturing (Toyota)
•	Estimated Time: 1 minute 30 seconds
•	What to Say:
"Our first example comes from manufacturing: Toyota. Every worker on a Toyota assembly line has access to a physical rope or button called the Andon Cord. If a worker spots even a tiny defect—or falls behind by a few seconds—they are expected to pull that cord, which immediately halts the entire production line.Now, in a traditional factory, stopping the line gets you fired. At Toyota, managers actually walk over and thank the worker for catching the problem before it moves downstream. By empowering the frontline to stop and fix problems immediately, Toyota eliminated massive rework and became the global benchmark for reliability."
•	Transition: "You might think, 'That works in a factory, but what about service?' Let’s look at hospitality."
________________________________________
Slide 5: Live Case Study 2 – Service (The Ritz-Carlton)
•	Estimated Time: 1 minute 30 seconds
•	What to Say:
"In the luxury hotel industry, The Ritz-Carlton has a famous rule: every single employee—from the general manager down to the housekeeping staff—is authorized to spend up to $2,000 per guest, per incident to fix a problem on the spot, without asking a manager for permission.Why? Because in customer service, bureaucracy kills quality. If a guest's suit isn't pressed in time for a wedding, a staff member can immediately buy them a new one or arrange express tailoring. By trusting employees with real authority, Ritz-Carlton turns potential disasters into lifelong customer loyalty."
•	Transition: "Now let’s see how this applies to tech innovation and large-scale industrial transformation."
